 $\\mathcal B,$ and we find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070757/example-5923.json" "file_sha256" "a08db35c798874ba605ce66dd1f5d04cc7f42f1b8307ec5fe5e6102290b27279"}] "usage" {"q-83617" [] "e-9906" [] "e-5922" [] "e-5923" [] "q-39858" []} "basis" 2525 "retractions" [[17592186184795 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$, where\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186184717 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}, {x_{1} - 3x_{2} + 2x_{3} = 0; 3x_{1} - 9x_{2} + 6x_{3} = 0$"] [17592186184018 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186184018 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 18\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = x^(^)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(x, x^(^)) &= ‖x - x^(^)‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - x^(^)_{1})^{2} + (x_{2} - x^(^)_{2})^{2} + (x_{3} - x^(^)_{3})^{2} + (x_{4} - x^(^)_{4})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-3 - (-3))^{2} + (5 - 5)^{2} + (-1 - 1)^{2} + (3 - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0 + 0 + 4 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186184055 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186184055 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}27 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 27\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{72}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$. Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$. So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= (0 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\cdot 0)/(\\sqrt{0^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}2\\right)= \\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle, as desired."] [17592186184019 :question/problem "Given the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186184019 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - 2R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$, and $x_3$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = 2x_2-3x_3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x_{2} - 3x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nis a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we let $A$ be a matrix whose columns equal the elements of $\\mathcal B$, and we find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}x \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186183192 17592186183193 17592186183194 17592186183195 17592186183196 17592186183660 17592186183661 17592186183662 17592186183663 17592186183664] "immutable_fields" [[17592186183191 :answer-field/correct] [17592186183191 :answer-field/id] [17592186183191 :answer-field/key] [17592186183191 :answer-field/type] [17592186183659 :answer-field/correct] [17592186183659 :answer-field/id] [17592186183659 :answer-field/key] [17592186183659 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
